.TH byobu-status-tmux 1 "4 Nov 2026" byobu "byobu"
.SH NAME
byobu\-status\-tmux \- gathers byobu's tmux status outside the tmux server

.SH DESCRIPTION
tmux runs every \fI#(command)\fP in its status line by forking the whole tmux server, about once a second, for each side of the status line and each attached client.  The cost of each fork grows with the server's memory, so a long\-lived server with deep scrollback can spend most of its time forking, and keystrokes lag behind.

\fBbyobu\-status\-tmux\fP avoids that.  \fBbyobu\-status\fP(1) starts one per tmux server, the first time tmux runs it; it then gathers the status once a second in its own process, publishes it in the tmux user options \fI@byobu_status_left\fP and \fI@byobu_status_right\fP, and replaces \fI#(byobu\-status tmux_left)\fP and \fI#(byobu\-status tmux_right)\fP in \fIstatus\-left\fP and \fIstatus\-right\fP with \fI#{E:@byobu_status_left}\fP and \fI#{E:@byobu_status_right}\fP, leaving the rest of those options alone.  It skips gathering while no client is attached, exits along with the tmux server, and on exit puts the \fI#()\fP commands back.

It needs tmux 2.9 or later; with older versions, tmux keeps running \fBbyobu\-status\fP directly.  To keep that behaviour on any version, create \fI$BYOBU_CONFIG_DIR/status\-tmux.disable\fP; a running \fBbyobu\-status\-tmux\fP notices within a second, puts the \fI#()\fP commands back and exits.

It is not meant to be run by hand.

.SH SEE ALSO
\fBbyobu\-status\fP(1), \fBtmux\fP(1)

